---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/docs.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Agents, API keys, and Git — Querial">
    <div class="space-y-8">
        <div>
            <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-querial-sky">For DevOps</p>
            <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mt-1">Agents, API keys, and Git</h1>
        </div>
        <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none space-y-6 [&>p]:text-muted-foreground [&>h2]:text-foreground">
            <h2>Agents and pools</h2>
            <p>
                Agents are registered processes, not anonymous functions you push a payload
                to. They start with a stable id, register into a <strong class="text-foreground">project-scoped</strong>
                pool, advertise capabilities (SQL, DuckDB, artifacts), poll for leases in
                that project, renew while running, and complete or fail. If an agent dies,
                the lease expires (after grace) and another eligible agent can take the work.
            </p>
            <p>
                Cron creates runs. Querial owns assignment. Eligible agents run DuckDB
                embedded — there is no separate DuckDB cluster. Agents do not need Git.
            </p>
            <p>
                These screens require CanOperate. Developers do not see Agents in nav.
                Control-plane worker hosts remain trusted when registered-agents mode is off.
            </p>

            <h2>API keys</h2>
            <p>
                Keys live under Workers in Workspace, not in Administration. Prefix
                <code class="text-foreground">qk_</code>, SHA-256 hash at rest, constant-time
                compare, shown in full once at create. Two kinds: project-scoped (any
                deployment in that project) and deployment-scoped (one deployment;
                mismatch is 404). Scopes: trigger_run, view_status, cancel_run, with
                optional environment restriction. Idempotency keys have a 24-hour TTL.
                A project setting can disable use of project-scoped keys without deleting
                them. A key cannot start or inspect another project's runs and cannot act
                as Platform admin.
            </p>
            <p>
                External trigger, status, and cancel use API-key auth, not a user session.
                JSON and multipart bodies, the trigger-contract, and Web Run now share
                one merge rule —
                <a href="/docs/devops-trigger" class="text-link hover:underline">Trigger API and Run now</a>.
                Developers cannot trigger Production via a key any more than they can in the UI.
            </p>

            <h2>Git remotes</h2>
            <p>
                Optional. Works with any standard Git host over the Git protocol. Auth is
                a username plus token stored in Querial — not host REST APIs, not pull-request
                automation. Publication must not become a secret dump. Workspace layout for
                packages is a folder tree, not a second database.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</DocsLayout>
